$ man ld 2>/dev/null | col -b | grep -n -A40 '^ *-l namespec' | head -60
401:     -l namespec
402-     --library=namespec
403-	 Add the archive or object file specified by namespec to the list of
404-	 files to link.	 This option may be used any number of times.  If
405-	 namespec is of the form :filename, ld will search the library path for
406-	 a file called filename, otherwise it will search the library path for
407-	 a file called libnamespec.a.
408-
409-	 On systems which support shared libraries, ld may also search for
410-	 files other than libnamespec.a.  Specifically, on ELF and SunOS
411-	 systems, ld will search a directory for a library called
412-	 libnamespec.so before searching for one called libnamespec.a.	(By
413-	 convention, a ".so" extension indicates a shared library.)  Note that
414-	 this behavior does not apply to :filename, which always specifies a
415-	 file called filename.
416-
417-	 The linker will search an archive only once, at the location where it
418-	 is specified on the command line.  If the archive defines a symbol
419-	 which was undefined in some object which appeared before the archive
420-	 on the command line, the linker will include the appropriate file(s)
421-	 from the archive.  However, an undefined symbol in an object appearing
422-	 later on the command line will not cause the linker to search the
423-	 archive again.
424-
425-	 See the -( option for a way to force the linker to search archives
426-	 multiple times.
427-
428-	 You may list the same archive multiple times on the command line.
429-
430-	 This type of archive searching is standard for Unix linkers.  However,
431-	 if you are using ld on AIX, note that it is different from the
432-	 behaviour of the AIX linker.
433-
434-     -L searchdir
435-     --library-path=searchdir
436-	 Add path searchdir to the list of paths that ld will search for
437-	 archive libraries and ld control scripts.  You may use this option any
438-	 number of times.  The directories are searched in the order in which
439-	 they are specified on the command line.  Directories specified on the
